"""Drivers: see what this machine has, and install the drivers that fit it.

A stick travels between machines, so this window is a report on the machine
the stick is plugged into right now rather than a list of packages. It knows
nothing about drivers itself. Everything comes from portlin-install, started
as a child process:

    portlin-install scan --json                     what hardware is here, what fits it
    portlin-install list --json --category Drivers  every driver entry, and whether it is installed
    portlin-install install|remove <ids>            through pkexec, read as the line protocol

So the window is a reader of JSON and of the `::step` / `::progress` /
`::warn` / `::reboot` / `::result` lines, the program that can be asked to
act as root stays one command with a fixed set of verbs, and first boot
and this window cannot disagree about what a machine needs.
"""

